   "text": {
    "L1": "A map is a small drawing of a much bigger place.",
    "L2": "The scale tells us how much real distance one centimetre stands for.",
    "L3": "To find real distance, multiply the map length by the scale.",
    "L4": "For example, 6 centimetres times 10 metres gives 60 metres.",
    "L5": "The answer is a real distance in metres or kilometres, not centimetres.",
    "L6": "Here is a common mistake: adding the map length and the scale.",
    "L7": "That is wrong. Five centimetres times 10 metres is 50 metres, not 15 metres.",
    "L8": "For a bending road, measure along the road because it is longer than a straight line.",
    "L9": "Now use the scale to answer the question."
   },
